{{also|Prudent}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|prudent}}, from {{der|en|fro|prudent}}, from {{der|en|la|prūdēns}}, contracted from {{m|la|prōvidēns||foresight}} ({{cog|en|providence}}), the past participle of {{m|la|prōvideō|t=I forsee}}. Unrelated to {{m|en|prude}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɹuːdənt/}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-prudent.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[sagacious|Sagacious]] in adapting means to ends; [[circumspect]] in action, or in determining any line of conduct.
#: {{syn|en|careful|considerate|discreet|Thesaurus:cautious}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1864
|author=Jules Verne
|title=A Journey to the Center of the Earth
|chapter=30
|url=http://ebooks.adelaide.edu.au/v/verne/jules/v52aw/chapter30.html
|passage=He did not hesitate what to do. It would be '''prudent''' to continue on to Omaha, for it would be dangerous to return to the train, which the Indians might still be engaged in pillaging.}}
#* '''1643''', {{w|John Milton}}, ''Doctrine and Discipline of Divorce''
#*: Moses established a grave and '''prudent''' law.
# Practically [[wise]], [[judicious]], [[shrewd]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:wise}}
#: {{ux|en|His '''prudent''' career moves reliably brought him to the top.}}
# [[frugal|Frugal]], [[economical]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:frugal}}
#: {{ant|en|extravagant}}
#: {{ux|en|Only '''prudent''' expenditure may provide quality within a restrictive budget.}}

